# Cloudflare Pages response headers. Replaces the next.config.ts headers() block,
# which static export can't emit. Same baseline security headers, applied to
# every path: clickjacking, MIME sniffing, and referrer leakage. (No CSP — see
# the note in the original next.config.ts: the inline theme-init script would
# need a per-request nonce/hash and a misconfigured CSP failing closed is a worse
# risk than its marginal benefit here.)
/*
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin

# The service worker must always be revalidated so a fresh build's worker is
# picked up promptly — a cached sw.js would strand installed users on an old
# version (the in-app "newer snapshot" prompt still depends on this fetch).
/sw.js
  Cache-Control: no-cache

# Public data API: /api/v1/* (static files generated by scripts/gen-api.mjs) and
# the bulk availability feed at the site root, /availability.json (read by
# muster.fusionspace.co). Cached ~10 min; the data refreshes on the ~hourly
# deploy, which also invalidates the edge copy.
#
# No Access-Control-* headers here, on purpose. Browser access is decided per
# request by the Pages Functions in functions/api/v1/_middleware.ts and
# functions/availability.json.ts (rule in lib/apiAccess.ts): fusionspace.co sites
# and the origins in API_ALLOWED_ORIGINS get Access-Control-Allow-Origin, other
# origins get 403. Those Functions serve the file through context.next(), which
# still applies the rules in this file, so Cache-Control below holds. A wildcard
# CORS line here would reach every response whenever the Functions don't run
# (for example if the daily Functions allowance runs out and the project fails
# open). Keep it out; lib/apiAccess.test.ts fails if one appears.
/availability.json
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=600

/api/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=600
